  "image_name": "290d2cdadf32bc063d3420f13380c0d6.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure combines a system sketch with a table of performance targets for 100‑gigabit Ethernet optical links. The lower diagram shows the basic signal path: a transmitting laser provides optical input power to a modulator, which converts a digital bit stream into an intensity‑modulated optical signal. That signal then travels through an optical fiber link and reaches a detector, where the received optical power determines detection performance. Labels for input power, output power levels, and detector power emphasize the power budget through the link.\n\nThe table above compares three applications—100GE CLR4, LR4, and ER4—mainly by transmission distance and the penalties the system must tolerate. As link length increases from 2 km to 10 km and then 30–40 km, link loss rises strongly, reaching 16 dB for ER4. The required extinction ratio also increases from about 4–5 dB to 8 dB, while the allowed power penalty drops from 3 dB to 0.6 dB. Receiver sensitivity appears similar for CLR4 and LR4, but becomes much more demanding for ER4, at around −21.4 dBm. Overall, the figure highlights how longer optical links require tighter power and receiver performance.",
